WEBVTT

00:00.830 --> 00:05.770
So we have f of x is equal to
negative x plus 4, and f of x

00:05.770 --> 00:08.460
is graphed right here on
our coordinate plane.

00:08.460 --> 00:11.690
Let's try to figure out
what the inverse of f is.

00:11.690 --> 00:14.770
And to figure out the inverse,
what I like to do is I set y, I

00:14.770 --> 00:18.590
set the variable y, equal to f
of x, or we could write that y

00:18.590 --> 00:22.130
is equal to negative x plus 4.

00:22.130 --> 00:25.040
Right now, we've solved
for y in terms of x.

00:25.040 --> 00:26.750
To solve for the inverse,
we do the opposite.

00:26.750 --> 00:29.600
We solve for x in terms of y.

00:29.600 --> 00:31.570
So let's subtract 4
from both sides.

00:31.570 --> 00:36.160
You get y minus 4 is
equal to negative x.

00:36.160 --> 00:39.000
And then to solve for x, we can
multiply both sides of this

00:39.000 --> 00:41.960
equation times negative 1.

00:41.960 --> 00:47.530
And so you get negative
y plus 4 is equal to x.

00:47.530 --> 00:50.390
Or just because we're always
used to writing the dependent

00:50.390 --> 00:52.770
variable on the left-hand side,
we could rewrite this as x is

00:52.770 --> 00:55.620
equal to negative y plus 4.

00:55.620 --> 00:58.300
Or another way to write it
is we could say that f

00:58.300 --> 01:06.840
inverse of y is equal
to negative y plus 4.

01:06.840 --> 01:09.470
So this is the inverse function
right here, and we've written

01:09.470 --> 01:13.360
it as a function of y, but we
can just rename the y as x

01:13.360 --> 01:14.950
so it's a function of x.

01:14.950 --> 01:16.080
So let's do that.

01:16.080 --> 01:23.120
So if we just rename this y as
x, we get f inverse of x is

01:23.120 --> 01:25.780
equal to the negative x plus 4.

01:25.780 --> 01:27.540
These two functions
are identical.

01:27.540 --> 01:30.730
Here, we just used y as the
independent variable, or

01:30.730 --> 01:31.760
as the input variable.

01:31.760 --> 01:34.660
Here we just use x, but they
are identical functions.

01:34.660 --> 01:37.840
Now, just out of interest,
let's graph the inverse

01:37.840 --> 01:40.220
function and see how it
might relate to this

01:40.220 --> 01:42.190
one right over here.

01:42.190 --> 01:44.250
So if you look at it,
it actually looks

01:44.250 --> 01:45.510
fairly identical.

01:45.510 --> 01:47.010
It's a negative x plus 4.

01:47.010 --> 01:48.395
It's the exact same function.

01:48.395 --> 01:51.630
So let's see, if we have-- the
y-intercept is 4, it's going

01:51.630 --> 01:52.950
to be the exact same thing.

01:52.950 --> 01:56.810
The function is
its own inverse.

01:56.810 --> 01:58.990
So if we were to graph it,
we would put it right

01:58.990 --> 01:59.930
on top of this.

02:02.560 --> 02:04.730
And so, there's a couple of
ways to think about it.

02:04.730 --> 02:07.530
In the first inverse function
video, I talked about how a

02:07.530 --> 02:10.390
function and their inverse--
they are the reflection

02:10.390 --> 02:12.030
over the line y equals x.

02:12.030 --> 02:14.220
So where's the line
y equals x here?

02:14.220 --> 02:16.445
Well, line y equals
x looks like this.

02:20.540 --> 02:25.710
And negative x plus 4 is
actually perpendicular to y is

02:25.710 --> 02:27.780
equal to x, so when you reflect
it, you're just kind of

02:27.780 --> 02:29.830
flipping it over, but it's
going to be the same line.

02:29.830 --> 02:32.670
It is its own reflection.

02:32.670 --> 02:34.470
Now, let's make sure that
that actually makes sense.

02:34.470 --> 02:38.700
When we're dealing with the
standard function right

02:38.700 --> 02:43.480
there, if you input a 2,
it gets mapped to a 2.

02:43.480 --> 02:48.750
If you input a 4, it
gets mapped to 0.

02:48.750 --> 02:50.370
What happens if you
go the other way?

02:50.370 --> 02:54.460
If you input a 2, well, 2
gets mapped to 2 either

02:54.460 --> 02:55.870
way, so that makes sense.

02:55.870 --> 02:59.180
For the regular function,
4 gets mapped to 0.

02:59.180 --> 03:02.320
For the inverse function,
0 gets mapped to 4.

03:02.320 --> 03:03.710
So it actually makes
complete sense.

03:03.710 --> 03:04.610
Let's think about
it another way.

03:04.610 --> 03:07.770
For the regular function-- let
me write it explicitly down.

03:07.770 --> 03:09.390
This might be obvious to
you, but just in case it's

03:09.390 --> 03:11.950
not, it might be helpful.

03:11.950 --> 03:14.440
Let's pick f of 5.

03:14.440 --> 03:18.020
f of 5 is equal to negative 1.

03:18.020 --> 03:23.900
Or we could say, the function f
maps us from 5 to negative 1.

03:23.900 --> 03:27.230
Now, what does f inverse do?

03:27.230 --> 03:31.190
What's f inverse of negative 1?

03:31.190 --> 03:33.325
f inverse of negative 1 is 5.

03:36.200 --> 03:41.000
Or we could say that f maps
us from negative 1 to 5.

03:41.000 --> 03:44.140
So once again, if you think
about kind of the sets, they're

03:44.140 --> 03:46.370
our domains and our ranges.

03:46.370 --> 03:49.070
So let's say that this is
the domain of f, this

03:49.070 --> 03:50.760
is the range of f.

03:50.760 --> 03:59.040
f will take us from
to negative 1.

03:59.040 --> 04:00.960
That's what the
function f does.

04:00.960 --> 04:04.920
And we see that f inverse takes
us back from negative 1 to 5.

04:04.920 --> 04:09.720
f inverse takes us back
from negative 1 to 5, just

04:09.720 --> 04:12.320
like it's supposed to do.

04:12.320 --> 04:15.200
Let's do one more of these.

04:15.200 --> 04:19.070
So here I have g of x is equal
to negative 2x minus 1.

04:19.070 --> 04:23.000
So just like the last problem,
I like to set y equal to this.

04:23.000 --> 04:25.340
So we say y is equal to g
of x, which is equal to

04:25.340 --> 04:27.740
negative 2x minus 1.

04:27.740 --> 04:29.950
Now we just solve for x.

04:29.950 --> 04:32.910
y plus 1 is equal
to negative 2x.

04:32.910 --> 04:34.920
Just added 1 to both sides.

04:34.920 --> 04:39.050
Now we can divide both sides of
this equation by negative 2,

04:39.050 --> 04:46.630
and so you get negative y over
2 minus 1/2 is equal to x, or

04:46.630 --> 04:52.420
we could write x is equal to
negative y over 2 minus 1/2, or

04:52.420 --> 04:56.260
we could write f inverse as a
function of y is equal to

04:56.260 --> 05:02.435
negative y over 2 minus 1/2, or
we can just rename y as x.

05:02.435 --> 05:08.270
And we could say that f inverse
of-- oh, let me careful here.

05:08.270 --> 05:09.250
That shouldn't be an f.

05:09.250 --> 05:11.300
The original function was
g , so let me be clear.

05:11.300 --> 05:21.850
That is g inverse of y is equal
to negative y over 2 minus 1/2

05:21.850 --> 05:24.340
because we started with a
g of x, not an f of x.

05:24.340 --> 05:26.120
Make sure we get our
notation right.

05:26.120 --> 05:31.010
Or we could just rename the y
and say g inverse of x is equal

05:31.010 --> 05:34.320
to negative x over 2 minus 1/2.

05:34.320 --> 05:35.140
Now, let's graph it.

05:35.140 --> 05:37.970
Its y-intercept
is negative 1/2.

05:37.970 --> 05:39.970
It's right over there.

05:39.970 --> 05:43.460
And it has a slope
of negative 1/2.

05:48.940 --> 05:52.760
Let's see, if we start at
negative 1/2, if we move over

05:52.760 --> 05:56.500
to 1 in the positive direction,
it will go down half.

05:56.500 --> 05:59.770
If we move over 1 again, it
will go down half again.

05:59.770 --> 06:01.650
If we move back-- so
it'll go like that.

06:01.650 --> 06:05.440
So the line, I'll try my
best to draw it, will

06:05.440 --> 06:07.830
look something like that.

06:07.830 --> 06:10.580
It'll just keep going, so it'll
look something like that, and

06:10.580 --> 06:13.170
it'll keep going in
both directions.

06:13.170 --> 06:15.400
And now let's see if this
really is a reflection over y

06:15.400 --> 06:21.910
equals x. y equals x looks
like that, and you can see

06:21.910 --> 06:22.750
they are a reflection.

06:22.750 --> 06:25.440
If you reflect this guy, if you
reflect this blue line, it

06:25.440 --> 06:27.220
becomes this orange line.

06:27.220 --> 06:30.885
But the general idea, you
literally just-- a function

06:30.885 --> 06:34.460
is originally expressed, is
solved for y in terms of x.

06:34.460 --> 06:35.530
You just do some algebra.

06:35.530 --> 06:38.750
Solve for x in terms of y, and
that's essentially your inverse

06:38.750 --> 06:41.120
function as a function of y,
but then you can rename

06:41.120 --> 06:43.700
it as a function of x.
